          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly presents her love for the little prince as true and explains that he failed to know it because of her own fault. This is a clear avowal of a loving emotional state.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows present love but does not itself promise future conduct, renew a relationship, or undertake an obligation. Commitments should not be imported from the rose's neighbouring remarks.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although the farewell is emotionally charged, the focal words are represented as a controlled disclosure within a calm explanation, not as a reflex-like cry or affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The core force of the focal expression is adequately captured as an avowal of love; the supplied evidence does not establish a distinct residual function beyond T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During the little prince's departure, the rose responds to his farewell after apologising and wishing him happiness. She calmly discloses that she loves him and says that her own conduct prevented him from knowing it.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no identifiable undertaking by the rose, so there is no commitment whose uptake can be assessed. The surrounding scene represents affection through her avowal and concealed tears, but that does not convert the focal words into a promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Chapter IX of Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince (The Little Prince). The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents present affection, but it does not independently establish any future relational commitment or provide explicit uptake of the love avowal by the little prince.\"}"
        }
